cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this project or its findings, please cite it as below."
title: "Flake Laundering: Does a Coding Agent Fix the Race or Weaken the Test?"
authors:
  - family-names: "Syed"
    given-names: "Zaid Ali"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-4313-1510"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-28
url: "https://github.com/zaidwhy/flake-laundering"
repository-code: "https://github.com/zaidwhy/flake-laundering"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  Two pre-registered studies, 229 scored runs of Claude Code (Sonnet 5.5) on web apps whose
  correct end-to-end test fails intermittently because of a real race. Under a plain "get CI
  green" brief the agent weakened the test in 0 of 60 runs and fixed the race in 60 of 60,
  including when the cause was split across lazily loaded modules, buried in a 44-file app, or
  met while shipping an unrelated feature. On tests that were genuinely wrong, the brief "do not
  change the tests" cut correct repairs from 9 of 10 to 0 of 10; asking instead "the test may be
  wrong or the app may be wrong; find out which" repaired 10 of 10 and weakened 0 of 40 correct
  tests.
keywords:
  - coding agents
  - flaky tests
  - reward hacking
  - end-to-end testing
  - race conditions
  - prompt design
  - pre-registration
